  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(|x-1|\\le 2)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1| & \\le 2 \\\\ -2\\le x-1 & \\le 2 \\\\ -1\\le x & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|<1)\\,⇒\\,(|x-1|\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $|x|<1,$ then $-1<x<3$ since $(-1,1)⊂(-1,3).$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|<3)\\,⇏\\,(|x-1|\\le 2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1| & =|-2-1| \\\\  & =3≰2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(0<x<2)\\,⇒\\,(|x-1|\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $0<x<2,$  then $-1\\le x\\le 3,$ since $(0,2)⊂[-1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
